  "problem": "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{32}$, ${a}_{43}$ and ${a}_{44}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form.",
